# Caldera River Tap — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera River Tap, the smallest, lowest waterworks of the
Holdfast — a low brass sluice gate spanning a river channel, a weir with a liftable gate in
an iron frame, with an undershot paddle wheel turning in the flow beside it — as a 3D voxel
model with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build it with `voxel-anim` (painting opaque
cells one operation at a time) as a rigged model that reads unmistakably as this simple
weir, turns its paddle wheel gently on its own, and lifts its gate to draw water on demand.
There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes what
the River Tap is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts list, joint
placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a sluice gate and paddle wheel need,
where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the Tap, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the Tap's front face looks toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front), and the river
  channel runs across the width (x). Up is +y.
- The Tap is a **static structure founded on the ground**: its frame sits flat on the ground
  at `y = 0`, spanning the channel, and does not move. Build the sluice gate and frame regular
  about the vertical centerplane; the **paddle wheel sits to one side**, beside the gate, so
  the model is deliberately **not fully mirror-symmetric** — that offset wheel is intended.
- The Tap is deliberately **small and low** — the smallest, lowest structure of the roster, a
  low weir hugging the channel, never tall or bulky. Nothing about it should read as powered,
  heavy, or machine-like beyond a simple gate and wheel.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same volume's
  coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Tap (the gate already seated
  in the frame, the paddle wheel already beside it in the channel).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Brass — primary plating | `#c8a24a` |
| Brass dark — shadow, underside | `#8a6d2e` |
| Iron — mechanisms, pipework, recesses | `#3a3836` |
| Accent fitting — verdigris (the accent region) | `#2f7d72` |

Do **not** use steel `#b8bcc2`, gold `#ffce54`, or white `#dfeaea` anywhere on the model.
Those are the colors a game paints onto Holdfast accent fittings at run time (see *The
accent region*); baking any of them into the model corrupts the recolor the build runs on
every accent voxel.

## What the River Tap is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the River Tap must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **low brass sluice gate** spanning a river channel — a **weir** with a **liftable gate**
  held in an **iron frame** across the channel, low and hugging the ground. Its shadowed
  seams and undersides are brass dark.
- A **liftable gate** in the frame that lifts to let water through and lowers to hold it back
  (see the animations).
- An **undershot paddle wheel** turning in the flow beside the gate — a bladed wheel set low
  in the channel, driven by the water passing under it.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the frame and
gate, how the weir is shaped, how many blades the paddle wheel carries and how it is mounted,
and how you break the Tap into rig parts. Nothing here prescribes a shape; the test rewards a
bold, characterful design that is unmistakably the River Tap and animates convincingly.

The River Tap is a **structure of the Holdfast — brass-and-iron machinery, not a creature**.
The tell is the low SLUICE GATE and the undershot PADDLE WHEEL: it is the smallest and
lowest structure, a simple gravity-fed weir founded on the ground, with nothing about it
reading as powered, heavy, tall, or alive.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time by finding every voxel of one reserved color and
repainting it. The fluid structures do not upgrade, so the River Tap's accent is **always**
painted to brass dark — but the build runs that recolor **unconditionally** on load, so the
region must still be authored exactly right. That reserved color is the **verdigris accent**
`#2f7d72`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **sluice gate frame** — and only it — in `#2f7d72`.
- Use `#2f7d72` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not a
  seam. Every voxel of it is treated as accent fitting.
- The frame must be **one clean, unbroken run** around the gate, and clearly **visible from
  more than one angle**.

Everything else — the weir, the gate itself, the paddle wheel, the recesses — keeps its own
color and is never repainted. Get this wrong and the Tap recolors wrong on load.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the
period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion reads right — a gentle turn at idle and a steady draw when it is working, never heavy.

- **`idle`** — sitting in the current (a self-playing idle, `loop = true`,
  `auto_play = true`). The paddle wheel turns **gently** in the current and the gate stays
  still, so the Tap reads as sitting in the river rather than drawing. It cycles on its own
  with no caller. Only the paddle wheel turns; the gate, frame, and weir stay fixed. It loops
  seamlessly.
- **`draw`** — drawing water (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The sluice gate
  **lifts** within its frame and the paddle wheel **spins up** as water is drawn through the
  weir, cycling steadily. This plays while the Tap is drawing, so it reads as **active but
  simple — gravity-fed, never heavy or powered** — plainly distinct from the gentle idle. The
  frame and weir stay planted.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a drip
at the gate), but you must not drop or contradict the required `idle` and `draw` animations,
by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a pivot), sculpt
each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`, place joints with
`define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and a
mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation; run
`voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png` previews)
between calls to judge each part against this brief — that the gate seats in the frame across
the channel, the paddle wheel sits low in the flow beside it, the frame reads as one unbroken
run, and the two animations read with the right contrast between sitting and drawing. Run
`voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is
built from — an unrendered part scores as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>`
renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed
at that instant to check the motion).
